<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Hardened Runtime entitlements for Kingfisher.

  This file is the smallest possible set of capabilities the
  application needs once the macOS hardened runtime is enabled.
  Each line is justified; an entitlement without a justification
  in this comment is a bug.

  Phase 47 audit (the short version):

    allow-jit                                   V8 and the WebAssembly engine.
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory            The same JIT pages.
    user-selected.read-write                    File dialogs.
    network.client                              GitHub, Lichess, the
                                                updater feed.
    network.server                              Local web server
                                                that ships the
                                                Next app to the
                                                desktop renderer.

  Not granted, and why:

    disable-library-validation   Library validation governs code loaded
                                 *into* a process: dylibs, native Node
                                 modules. Kingfisher loads none: the
                                 companion uses Node built-ins (node:sqlite
                                 included) and the updater hands its archive
                                 to macOS's own update engine. Managed
                                 engines are separate processes the companion
                                 spawns, and a spawned binary is checked by
                                 its own signature, not by the parent's
                                 entitlements. Phase 36 granted this for the
                                 engines; it never applied to them.
    allow-dyld-environment-variables
                                 Only meaningful with the one above.
    debugger, get-task-allow     Never in a release build.

  The trust model for engines is docs/adr/0041-engine-trust-and-the-
  sandbox-we-do-not-have.md. the packaged engine gate (desktop:engines) runs
  every managed engine inside the signed, hardened bundle and is the
  evidence that this set is sufficient.

  If a future feature needs another entitlement, add it here
  *and* in this comment in the same commit. The release gate
  refuses a build whose entitlements diverge from this file.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!--
      WebAssembly JIT. The Stockfish engine Kingfisher ships is
      a JIT-compiled WASM build; without `allow-jit` and
      `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` the hardened runtime
      refuses to allocate the executable memory pages WASM
      needs to compile. Both must be present; one without the
      other crashes the engine on first move.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      File dialogs. A user-selected PGN, a chosen SQLite
      collection, a Syzygy directory, an engine binary — all
      of these are paths the user just chose. The user-selected
      read-write entitlement is the smallest scope Apple
      exposes for that case; it does not give us arbitrary
      file access, only access to the things the user has
      explicitly selected.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Network. The renderer reaches Lichess, GitHub (for the
      auto-update feed), and other chess data sources; the
      shell starts a local HTTP server that serves the bundled
      Next app to the sandboxed renderer. The renderer never
      binds a public port; the local server is reachable only
      on 127.0.0.1. We do not need any further network
      entitlement (no app-sandbox, no special iCloud, no
      incoming from the network).
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
